WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:06.480
Long-term forecasts combine historical earthquake records, prehistoric fault evidence, and tectonic

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:10.780
setting. Recurrence and potential event sizes help estimate hazard over an

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:15.280
interval. Hazard is generally high near many active plate boundaries, including

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:17.260
subduction zones and collision belts.

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:21.440
New Madrid and Charleston illustrate that some intraplate areas also have

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:25.460
important hazard. A hazard map describes an expected shaking measure or

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:27.360
probability over a defined time.

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:29.160
Read its legend and date;

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.920
a historical forecast map is not a newly issued forecast.

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:37.260
A seismic gap is a segment with less earthquake activity than

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:40.060
neighboring segments or than expected from its history.

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:43.880
In the chapter example, some quiet segments were interpreted as locked

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:47.580
and accumulating strain. Quiet does not automatically mean safe.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:51.440
Nor does a gap establish exactly when, where, and how large

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:52.740
the next earthquake will be.

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:55.700
A creeping segment and a locked segment can behave differently.

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:59.860
Combine deformation measurements with earthquake history rather than treating a lack

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:01.740
of dots as a complete explanation.

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:07.160
The Parkfield exercise used repeated events to anticipate another earthquake during

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:10.900
a specified window; the eventual 2004 event occurred later.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:13.480
An average interval is not a clock.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:18.860
Monitoring small motions, tilt, or possible foreshocks can inform research, but

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:22.220
there is no reliable method for predicting a specific earthquake’s exact

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:26.260
time, location, and magnitude. An early warning detects an earthquake that

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:29.820
has already begun and can alert places before stronger shaking arrives.

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:33.080
It is different from predicting the event before rupture starts.
